Provenance & retained upstream notice — self.llama/
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

self.llama/ is a vendored tree of llama.cpp
(https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp), pinned at upstream commit
e8f19cc0ad70a243c8012bf17b4be601abfc8ea2 (2026-07-16), originally licensed
MIT. It carries local patches on top of stock upstream — see "Local
modifications" below; it is not a byte-for-byte mirror, despite this
NOTICE's earlier wording having claimed otherwise. It was previously carried
as a separate GitLab project (selfshipyard/selfai/self.llama) and consumed
here via a git submodule; that indirection bought nothing (zero yard commits
on top of upstream, no independent issues) and caused real build friction
(empty/uninitialized checkouts, submodule-init pain in CI), so it was folded
in directly as a vendored subdirectory (`self.llamolotl#8`, 2026-07-10),
matching how every other upstream dependency in this subgroup
(self.code-eval, self.language-eval, self.curator, self.faster-whisper) is
vendored.

Why fully vendored: self.ai licenses the combined work under GPLv3. MIT is
one-way compatible with GPLv3, so the upstream code is included here under
GPLv3 while its original MIT license text and attribution are retained in
LICENSE.llama-cpp-mit. A GPLv3 combined work cannot be contributed back to an
MIT upstream, so self.llama/ is a hard vendor: we do not track or push to the
original project. self.llamolotl is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or
connected to the ggml-org/llama.cpp project.

Re-vendor history: originally pinned at 699bb0e95b1befd66c64456193a0dd958a96455e
(added 2026-04-22). Re-vendored to e8f19cc0ad70a243c8012bf17b4be601abfc8ea2
(2026-07-16) to pick up Gemma 4 (`gemma4`/`gemma4-iswa`) architecture support —
see `context/kits/cavekit-llama-revendor.md` for the full process and the
next re-vendor's runbook.

Local modifications (on top of the e8f19cc0 pin): `conversion/qwen.py`'s
`_LinearAttentionVReorderBase._reorder_v_heads` is patched to duck-type LoRA
tensors (checks for a `get_lora_A_B` method and recurses into the correct
A/B component instead of calling `.reshape()`/`.permute()` directly on a
`LoraTorchTensor`), needed for LoRA-adapter GGUF conversion on delta-net/
linear-attention (Qwen3-Next-family) models — confirmed still necessary at
this pin: upstream's own `LoraTorchTensor.reshape()`/`.permute()` explicitly
don't support the multi-dimension split-and-swap this function performs.
Originally added 2026-04-01 (as `699bb0e`, back when self.llama was still its
own GitLab project) with Claude assistance; ported forward to its new
location across the `conversion/` package refactor as part of this
re-vendor. See `cavekit-llama-revendor.md` R2 for the investigation.

`tools/server/server-models.cpp` / `.h`, `common/common.h`, and
`common/arg.cpp` are patched to add VRAM-aware model eviction to the router
server (new flags `--models-vram-aware` [default: on], `--models-vram-margin-mb`
[default: 512], `--models-vram-overhead-pct` [default: 20]): before a load, the
router now proactively evicts LRU resident model(s) if the incoming model's
estimated VRAM footprint would not fit in currently-free GPU memory, layered
on top of (not replacing) the pre-existing `--models-max` count-based limit.
Free VRAM is queried via an `nvidia-smi` shellout rather than a CUDA API
call, because the router process is deliberately kept off the GPU itself
(server.cpp: "router server never loads a model and must not touch the
GPU" / must not create a CUDA primary context) — this mirrors the existing
`nvidia-smi` precedent in `api/state.py`'s `_check_gpu()`. A model's
footprint is estimated from its on-disk GGUF file size(s) (not parsed GGUF
metadata), inflated by a configurable overhead percentage as a coarse proxy
for KV-cache/compute-buffer usage. Both queries degrade gracefully to a
no-op (falling back to the count-based limit alone) rather than guessing:
unknown free VRAM is never treated as zero, and an unresolvable model
footprint (e.g. a remote HF-repo preset not yet cached locally) is never
treated as zero footprint. Added self.llamolotl#22, 2026-07-18; see that
issue and the commit message for the full design rationale and limitations.
The generated `--help` text for these three flags means
`tools/server/README.md`'s auto-generated options table (marked "do NOT
modify it manually", built by `llama-gen-docs`) is stale until the next
regen — not done here, no local build available.

Removed from the vendored tree (confirmed unreferenced by
tools/server/CMakeLists.txt and every other build step this repo actually
uses):
- tools/server/bench/ — a standalone Python load-testing script (unrelated to
  the tools/llama-bench/ binary, which self.llamolotl's Dockerfile does use
  and keeps). Different content than what was here at the 699bb0e pin, same
  reasoning for dropping it.

The webui content removed at the original fold-in (tools/server/webui/,
public_legacy/, public_simplechat/, themes/, the chat-*.sh/.mjs CLI
examples) no longer exists at this pin in that form at all — upstream
rebuilt the web UI as a full SvelteKit app under tools/ui/, now a hard link
dependency of llama-server-impl (a new `llama-ui` CMake target) rather than
optionally-embedded static assets. It could not be stripped from the tree
the way the old webui was without patching tools/CMakeLists.txt's
`add_subdirectory(ui)` — a real local modification beyond a squash-import,
and one more thing to carry across every future re-vendor. Instead it is
vendored in full (dead weight, but zero additional source modification) and
disabled at build time via `-DLLAMA_BUILD_UI=OFF -DLLAMA_USE_PREBUILT_UI=OFF`
in both Dockerfiles, which skips both the npm build and the prebuilt-asset
download — no Node/npm in the image, no UI served. See
`cavekit-llama-revendor.md` R2 for the reasoning and R4 for the build
verification.

